===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|right}}, {{m|enm|righte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rihte}}, {{m|ang|rehte|t=right; rightly; due; directly; straight}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*rehta}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*rehtaz|t=right; straight}}.

====Adverb====
{{wikidata lexeme|L333560}}
{{en-adv|-}}

# On the [[right#Adjective|right]] side.
# Towards the right side.
# [[exactly|Exactly]], [[precisely]].
#: {{ux|en|The arrow landed '''right''' in the middle of the target.}}
#: {{ux|en|Luckily we arrived '''right''' at the start of the film.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=Then there came a reg'lar terror of a sou'wester same as you don't get one summer in a thousand, and blowed the shanty flat and ripped about half of the weir poles out of the sand. We spent consider'ble money getting 'em reset, and then a swordfish got into the pound and tore the nets all to slathers, '''right''' in the middle of the squiteague season.}}
# [[immediately|Immediately]], [[directly]].
#: {{ux|en|Can't you see it? It's '''right''' beside you!}}
#: {{ux|en|Tom was standing '''right''' in front of the TV, blocking everyone's view.}}
#: {{ux|en|You come '''right''' home this instant!}}
# {{lb|en|British|US|dialect}} [[very|Very]], [[extremely]], [[quite]].
#: {{ux|en|I made a '''right''' stupid mistake there, didn't I?}}
#: {{ux|en|I stubbed my toe a week ago and it still hurts '''right''' much.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Jeff Torrington|title=Swing Hammer Swing!|page=255
|passage=Kids nowadays were a '''right''' thrillproof bunch. The Armoury Section had, unexpectedly, proved to be a real moodclunker.}}
## {{lb|en|dated|still used in some titles}} To a great extent or degree.
##: {{ux|en|Sir, I am '''right''' glad to meet you &hellip;}}
##: {{ux|en|Members of the Queen's Privy Council are styled The '''Right''' Honourable for life.}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''Right''' Reverend Monsignor Guido Sarducci.}}
##* {{RQ:Jack Straw|IV|79|Sith mercie in a Prince reſembleth '''right''',<br>The gladſome ſunne-ſhine in a winters day, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=13|passage=He b'iled '''right''' over, and the tongue-lashing he give that boss Right Liver beat anything I ever listened to. There was heap of Scriptur' language in it, and more brimstone than you'd find in a match factory.}}
# According to fact or truth; [[actually]]; [[truly]]; [[really]].
# {{senseid|en|correctly-properly-etc}} In a [[correct]] or [[proper]] [[manner]].
#: {{ux|en|Do it '''right''' or don't do it at all.}}
#: {{ux|en|Nothing is going '''right''' for me lately.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In the US, the word "right" is used as an adverb meaning "very, quite" in most of the major dialect areas, including the Southern US, Appalachia, New England{{,}} and the Midwest, though the usage is not part of standard US English. In the UK also it is not part of the standard language but is regarded as stereotypical of the dialects of northern England, though it occurs in other dialects also.

