{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱel-|id1=incline|*(s)kelH-}}
From {{der|en|enm|halpeni}}, {{m|enm|halfpeni|t=English coin worth half a penny; coin used in biblical times; small foreign coin}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=hal-penī|pos=n|id=MED19861}}</ref> and then either:

* from {{m|enm|half|t=half}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=half|pos=adj|id=MED19825}}</ref> (from {{inh|en|ang|healf}};<ref>{{R:Anglo-Saxon Dictionary|entry=healf|page=518|column=2}}</ref> ultimate etymology unknown, possible from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱel-|id=incline|t=to incline}} or {{m|ine-pro|*(s)kelH-|t=to cut; to separate, split}}) + {{m|enm|peni}}, {{m|enm|penni|t=English coin; coin used in biblical times; foreign coin; (''especially in the plural'') cash, money; pennyweight}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=penī|pos=n|id=MED32818}}</ref> (from {{der|en|ang|penning}},<ref>{{R:Anglo-Saxon Dictionary|entry=pening|page=772|column=2}}</ref> from {{der|en|gem-pro|*panningaz|t=coin, penny}}; further etymology uncertain); or
* from Late {{der|en|ang|halpene}}, {{m|ang|halpenige}} (which can also be construed as early Middle English), from {{m|ang|healf}} + {{m|ang|penning}} (see above).

{{surf|en|half|penny}}.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1283863034|date=September 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

