# {{lb|en|coins|historical|also|attributive}}
## {{non-gloss|Senses chiefly with the {{lg|plural}} form {{m|en|halfpennies}}.}}
### {{senseid|en|UK coin}}{{lb|en|UK}} A [[discontinued#Adjective|discontinued]] [[English#Adjective|English]] (later [[British#Adjective|British]]) [[coin#Noun|coin]] worth [[half#Noun|half]] of one [[penny#Noun|penny]] ([[old#Adjective|old]] or [[new#Adjective|new]]).
###: {{synonyms|en|½d<q:pre-decimal abbreviation>|½p<q:decimal abbreviation>}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|act=III|scene=ii|page=197|column=2|passage=There vvere none principal, they vvere all like one another, as '''halfe pence''' are, euerie one fault ſeeming monſtrous, til his fellovv-fault came to match it.}}
###* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=V|book=XIII|chapter=Containing a Scene of Distress, which will Appear Very Extraordinary to Most of Our Readers|page=73|passage=[T]here are Thouſands vvho vvould not have contributed a ſingle '''Halfpenny''', as indeed he did not in Effect, for he made no Tender of any thing; and therefore as the others thought proer to make no Demand, he kept his Money in his Pocket.}}
###* {{RQ:Irving Goldsmith|edition=1st|volume=I|chapter=Biography of Oliver Goldsmith|page=53|passage=Whatever means he used in making his way to London, it is certain he made his entrance there with but a few '''halfpence''' in his pocket.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=a. '''1886''' (date written)|title=[[w:Christmas Is Coming|Christmas is Coming]]|newversion=quoted in|2ndauthor=[[w:Georgina Frederica Jackson|Georgina F[rederica] Jackson]], compiler|chapter2=Rhymes and Sayings. [Nursery Rhymes and Children’s Jingles.]|editor2=w:Charlotte Sophia Burne|title2=Shropshire Folk-lore: A Sheaf of Gleanings.{{nb...|[...] From the Collections of Georgina F. Jackson.}}|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner & Co.]],{{nb...|57 & 59, Ludgate Hill. Shrewsbury: Adnitt & Naunton. Chester: Minshull & Meeson.}}|year2=1886|volume_plain2=part III|page2=571|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=YhwAAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA571|oclc2=5148914|passage=Christmas is coming, the geese are getting fat, / Please to put a penny in the old man's hat, / If you haven't got a penny, a '''halfpenny''' will do, / If you haven't got a '''halfpenny'''—God bless you!}}
### {{lb|en|by extension|Ireland|Scotland}} A [[similar#Adjective|similar]] coin [[formerly]] [[use#Verb|used]] in [[Ireland]] and [[Scotland]].
## {{non-gloss|Senses chiefly with the plural form {{m|en|halfpence}}.}}
### {{lb|en|UK}} An [[amount#Noun|amount]] of [[money#Noun|money]] that the English (later British) coin was [[worth#Adjective|worth]].
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=IV|scene=ii|page=138|column=2|passage=There ſhall be in England, ſeuen '''halfe peny''' Loaues ſold for a peny: the three hoop'd pot, ſhall haue ten hoopes, and I vvill make it Fellony to drink ſmall Beere.}}
###* {{RQ:Goldsmith Citizen of the World|volume=II|page=v|passage=My earlieſt vviſh vvas to eſcape unheeded through life; but I have been ſet up for '''half-pence''', to fret and ſcamper at the end of my chain.}}
###* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|chapter=Dobbin of Ours|page=33|passage=[I]t was bruited abroad that he was admitted into Dr. Swishtail's academy upon what are called 'mutual principles'—that is to say, the expenses of his board and schooling were defrayed by his father, in goods not money; {{...}} The jokes were frightful, and merciless against him. {{...}} Another would set a sum—"If a pound of mutton-candles cost sevenpence-'''halfpenny''', how much must Dobbin cost?" and a roar would follow from all the circle of young knaves, usher and all, who rightly considered that the selling of goods by retail is a shameful and infamous practice, meriting the contempt and scorn of all real gentlemen.}}
### {{lb|en|by extension|Ireland|Scotland}} An amount of money that the [[Irish#Adjective|Irish]] or [[Scottish#Adjective|Scottish]] coin was worth.
# {{lb|en|by extension}}
